  "member_id": "pearce-mark",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B003",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B043",
    "B028"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks remain the clearest final case because a distinctive physical principle, magic-wavelength confinement, enabled an instrument whose systematic control, reproducibility, and precision have been tested across independent laboratories. It is not merely an incremental clock improvement: it created a durable measurement capability for timekeeping and fundamental tests. On the record before us, Katori's originating concept and Ye's defining experimental and metrological development form a compact, credible two-person allocation.",
    "case_against": "The decisive remaining question is historical scope. The international optical-clock community made major contributions to transitions, comparisons, systematic-error control, and applications. We should award the invention and defining development of optical lattice clocks, not imply that two individuals created the whole optical-clock enterprise. If review finds another indispensable contributor to the original concept or first decisive realization, this allocation would need reconsideration.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the magic-wavelength lattice-clock principle has produced independently reproducible precision measurement, while warning against extending credit to all modern optical timekeeping.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts. As an experimentalist, I put particular weight on the fact that the claimed advance has become a robust capability outside its originating laboratories. Kroll's boundary is also the correct one for a final citation: its strength lies in the lattice-clock principle and its defining realization, not in an undifferentiated account of later optical-clock progress."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson identifies Katori's magic-wavelength concept and Ye's independent experimental and metrological development as a clean core, subject to historical review of the wider precision-metrology community.",
        "response": "This supports retaining the Katori-Ye configuration rather than widening the award speculatively. The case combines a sharply stated physical idea with a mature experimental platform, but Johansson is right that attribution must be tested against the precise history of the invention and defining development. The current citation is deliberately narrow enough to make that test meaningful."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson regards spherical-aberration correction in electron microscopy as a transformative instrument achievement but identifies a difficult division of credit among conception, practical realization, and demonstration.",
        "response": "I continue to regard B043 as a strong alternative: it exemplifies the opening of a new observational window that instrument building can achieve. However, its credit chain remains less resolved on the supplied record. B003 presently has the more defensible connection between a central concept, its defining experimental development, and the proposed recipients."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical review establishes that Katori and Ye alone are indispensable recipients for a citation confined to optical lattice clocks.",
      "How the final citation can preserve the distinction between lattice-clock physics and the broader optical-clock enterprise."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict requiring recusal is apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
